/spawn --parallel -o x.md a --- b no longer throws the path away. It's refused before any worker starts, and the refusal names -o and your path. It's committed and the full gate passed: cargo test exit 0, clippy with -D warnings, fmt --check. After a last edit that only removed a test, I re-ran fmt, clippy and the spawn tests, not the whole suite.

What you see now:
```
-o x.md can't be used with --parallel: the tasks would all need their own file. Nothing was run. Remove -o x.md to run the tasks without saving, or save each one with /spawn -o <file> <task>
```
If --force is also on the line, the message says "Remove -o x.md and --force", so the next try doesn't hit the "force without -o" refusal. A path with spaces is shown in quotes, the way -o reads it.

Changes:
• The message is built in src/commands_spawn_args.rs. In commands_spawn.rs the --parallel branch now returns it instead of setting output_path: None. That file went from 4621 to 4624 lines, under its 4629 cap.
• One new test checks the full message for both word orders, --force before and after -o, and a quoted path with a space.
• The suggested fix is checked too: removing the words the message names gives a line that runs tasks a and b with no file and no refusal. A line with --parallel and no -o still works as before.
• Recall control: I disabled the check (1 marker, counted before the run) and the new tests failed by name. After restoring it (0 markers) they passed.
• Docs: one sentence added to the -o paragraph in docs/src/usage/commands.md.

A mess I made and cleaned up: I had also written a test that runs the real /spawn handler. While the check was disabled for the recall control, it really started two parallel workers in the repo: two worktrees under .yoyo/worktrees/ and a run record. git add -A then put the worktrees into my commit. They held no work, so I deleted them, amended the commit back to the four task files, and dropped that test. The existing an_unclosed_quote_spawns_nothing test already covers "a refused line starts nothing". It has the same risk if the check it guards ever breaks, so I filed #1225 for it, since this push closes #1224.

The only #1224 references in the diff are code comments; nothing users see points at it.
